            "content": "### 5-Element Architecture of an Audit Draft Paragraph\n\nIn the Indian Audit & Accounts Department (IA&AD), a Draft Paragraph (DP) submitted to Headquarters for inclusion in the Comptroller & Auditor General's Audit Report must adhere strictly to the 5 standard elements:\n\n1. **Title / Subject Line**: Concise, objective, capturing the quantified financial loss or irregularity without emotional language.\n2. **Context / Introduction**: Background of the scheme, project, or auditee institution, sanction amount, and administrative authority.\n3. **Criteria**: The legal, financial, or codal baseline that was breached (e.g. GFR 2017 Rule 149, CPWA Code Para 10.2.1, or specific scheme guidelines).\n4. **Condition / Audit Finding**: The factual irregularity observed during field audit, backed by incontrovertible documentary evidence. Must clearly quantify: Avoidable expenditure, Infructuous outlay, Loss of revenue, or Undue benefit to contractor.\n5. **Cause & Effect**: *Cause*: Why the deviation occurred (lack of internal controls, failure of supervisory monitoring, collusion). *Effect*: The measurable detriment to the public exchequer or intended beneficiaries.\n6. **Recommendation & Departmental Reply**: Constructive systemic remedy to prevent recurrence, followed by objective refutation of the department's justification.",
