            "chapterNum": 1,
            "title": "Definition, type of audits",
            "content": "### Definitions and Types of Public Sector Audits\n\nPublic sector auditing as conducted by the Comptroller and Auditor General of India is categorized into three primary disciplines and several specialized streams according to the CAG's Auditing Standards and Regulations on Audit and Accounts 2020:\n\n1. **Financial Audit**:\n   - *Objective*: To determine whether the financial statements of an entity (e.g. Union/State Finance Accounts, Autonomous Body accounts) are presented fairly in all material respects in accordance with the applicable financial reporting framework.\n   - *Key Deliverables*: Audit Opinion (Unqualified, Qualified, Adverse, or Disclaimer of Opinion) and Separate Audit Reports (SAR).\n2. **Compliance Audit**:\n   - *Objective*: To assess whether the activities, financial transactions, and information comply in all material respects with the authorities (Constitution, parliamentary acts, statutory rules, GFR 2017, and delegated powers) that govern the auditee.\n   - *Focus*: Regularity (adherence to formal laws/rules) and Propriety (adherence to sound public financial principles).\n3. **Performance Audit**:\n   - *Objective*: An independent, objective, and reliable examination of whether government undertakings, systems, programs, or organizations are functioning in accordance with the principles of Economy, Efficiency, and Effectiveness (the '3Es').\n   - *3Es Defined*:\n     - *Economy*: Minimizing the cost of resources employed while maintaining appropriate quality.\n     - *Efficiency*: Maximizing output from given resources or minimizing input for given output.\n     - *Effectiveness*: Achieving the intended program objectives and desired outcomes.\n4. **Thematic Audit**:\n   - Audit focusing on a specific subject, issue, or scheme executed across multiple departments or jurisdictions simultaneously.\n5. **Information Systems (IS) Audit**:\n   - Evaluating IT governance, general controls, application controls, data integrity, and security across automated government systems (e.g. IFMS, PFMS, GSTN).",
