            "title": "2.2 Relational Databases: Understanding integrity constraints, primary and foreign keys, and data manipulation techniques",
            "content": "### Relational Model: Integrity Constraints and Key Structures\n\n1. **Relational Database Structure**:\n   - Relation (Table), Tuples (Rows/Records), Attributes (Columns/Fields), Cardinality (number of rows), Degree (number of columns).\n2. **Database Keys**:\n   - *Candidate Key*: Minimal set of attributes that uniquely identifies each tuple.\n   - *Primary Key*: Candidate key chosen by database designer as primary identifier (must be NOT NULL and UNIQUE).\n   - *Foreign Key*: Attribute in one table that references the primary key of another table, creating a relational link.\n   - *Composite Key*: Primary key composed of two or more attributes.\n3. **Integrity Constraints**:\n   - *Entity Integrity*: Primary key cannot contain NULL values.\n   - *Referential Integrity*: Foreign key value must either match an existing primary key value in referenced table or be NULL (prevents orphaned records).\n   - *Domain Integrity*: Values in column must conform to defined data type, format, and range.\n4. **Database Views and Action Queries**:\n   - *View*: Virtual table based on a SELECT query; provides data abstraction and security (restricting user access to sensitive columns).\n   - *Action Queries*: Queries that modify data (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE).",
            "gist": "Relational model relies on Entity Integrity (Primary key != NULL) and Referential Integrity (Foreign key must match parent or be NULL). Views provide secure virtual tables.",
            "crux": "1. Entity integrity prohibits NULL primary keys.\n2. Referential integrity prevents orphaned records across linked tables.\n3. Views create virtual windows over sensitive tables for secure audit access.",
