            "title": "3.12 Ethical AI Usage: Principles of ethical AI deployment in governance and auditing",
            "content": "### Ethical AI Framework in Governance and Public Audit\n\n1. **Core Ethical Principles (NITI Aayog & CAG Framework)**:\n   - *Safety & Reliability*: AI systems must undergo thorough regression testing to prevent unintended public harm.\n   - *Equality & Inclusivity*: Training datasets must represent diverse demographic cross-sections to prevent bias against disadvantaged citizens.\n   - *Transparency & Auditability*: System architectures and decision weights must be open to independent scrutiny and audit verification.\n   - *Protection of Privacy*: Adherence to DPDP Act 2023 principles of purpose limitation and data minimization.\n   - *Accountability*: The ultimate moral and legal liability for decisions rests with human administrators, not autonomous algorithms.",
            "gist": "Ethical AI mandates fairness, safety, explainability, privacy protection, and human accountability in all government and auditing implementations.",
            "crux": "1. Explainability (XAI) is essential to validate audit conclusions.\n2. Human administrators retain ultimate legal accountability.\n3. Training data must be audited for algorithmic bias.",
