            "title": "1. Fundamental Rules & Supplementary Rules (Part I): (a) General Conditions of Service",
            "content": "### 1. Fundamental Rules & Supplementary Rules (Part I): (a) General Conditions of Service\n\nThe **Fundamental Rules (FR)**, applicable to civil servants across the Central Government and adapted by State Governments, govern the core legal conditions of service, pay determination, liens, and cadre management. Prescribed under the constitutional authority of **Article 309**, the general rules spanning **FR 1 to FR 26** establish the legal and financial boundaries of public employment.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Crucial Statutory Definitions (FR 9)\nMastery of definitions under FR 9 is essential for bill passing, audit, and service verification:\n- **FR 9(2) - Cadre**: The strength of a service or a part of a service sanctioned as a separate unit.\n- **FR 9(6) - Duty**: Duty includes:\n  1. Service as a probationer or apprentice, provided it is followed by confirmation.\n  2. Joining time.\n  3. A course of training or instruction authorized by the Government.\n  4. Time spent attending an obligatory departmental examination.\n- **FR 9(13) - Lien**: The title of a government servant to hold substantively, either immediately or on the termination of a period or periods of absence, a permanent post, including a tenure post, to which he has been appointed substantively.\n- **FR 9(20) - Overseas Pay**: Pay granted to a government servant in consideration of the fact that he is serving in a country other than his country of domicile.\n- **FR 9(21)(a) - Pay**: The amount drawn monthly by a government servant as:\n  1. Basic pay in the Pay Matrix level sanctioned for the post held substantively or in an officiating capacity.\n  2. Any special pay or personal pay.\n  3. Any other emoluments specially classed as pay by the President.\n- **FR 9(25) - Special Pay**: An addition of the nature of pay to the emoluments of a post or of a government servant, granted in consideration of the specially arduous nature of the duties or a specific addition to the work or responsibility.\n- **FR 9(27) - Subsistence Grant**: A monthly grant made to a government servant who is not in receipt of pay or leave-salary (e.g., during suspension under Rule 10 of CCS CCA Rules).\n\n---\n\n### 2. General Conditions of Service (FR 10 to FR 18)\n- **FR 10: Medical Certificate of Fitness**: No person may be substantively appointed to a permanent post in government service without a medical certificate of health signed by a designated medical authority.\n- **FR 11: Whole Time at Disposal of Government**: Unless expressly provided otherwise, the whole time of a government servant is at the disposal of the Government which pays him. He may be employed in any manner required by proper authority without claim for additional remuneration.\n- **FR 13 & 14: Acquisition, Suspension, and Termination of Lien**:\n  - A government servant acquires a lien on a post on substantive appointment.\n  - A lien cannot be suspended or terminated while the officer is on duty, on leave, or on joining time.\n  - Lien may be suspended under FR 14 if appointed in a substantive capacity to a permanent post outside the cadre, or during foreign service.\n  - A government servant's lien cannot be terminated under any circumstances without his written consent if the result would be to leave him without a lien or a suspended lien on a permanent post.\n- **FR 15: Transfers**: A government servant may be transferred from one post to another, provided that his pay is not reduced, except on disciplinary grounds under CCS (CCA) Rules or on his written request.\n- **FR 17 & 17-A: Reckoning Pay and Unexcused Absence**:\n  - **FR 17(1)**: A government servant begins to draw pay and allowances of a post from the date he assumes duties, and ceases to draw them from the date he relinquishes charge.\n  - **FR 17(2)**: An officer absent from duty without grant of leave ceases to be entitled to pay and allowances.\n  - **FR 17-A (Dies-Non)**: Unauthorized absence, strike participation, or concerted refusal to work results in an interruption of service (**dies-non**), entailing forfeiture of pay and allowances, postponement of increment, and exclusion from qualifying service for pension.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Principles of Pay Fixation and Increments (FR 22 to FR 26)\n1. **FR 22(I)(a)(1) - Promotion with Higher Responsibilities**:\n   - When a government servant is promoted or appointed to a post involving duties and responsibilities of greater importance, his initial pay in the Level of the higher post is fixed by:\n     - Granting **one notional increment** in the Level of the lower post.\n     - Locating the resulting figure in the Level of the higher post in the Pay Matrix. If an identical figure exists, pay is fixed at that Cell; if no such Cell exists, pay is fixed at the **next higher Cell**.\n   - *Option under FR 22(I)(a)(1)*: The employee has the statutory right to opt to have pay refixed from the date of his next annual increment in the lower post.\n2. **FR 22(I)(a)(2) - Promotion without Higher Responsibilities**:\n   - Pay is fixed at the Cell equal to pay in the lower post, or at the next higher Cell if no equal Cell exists (without granting a notional increment).\n3. **FR 26: Rules Governing Annual Increments**:\n   - Qualifying periods counting for annual increment:\n     1. All periods of duty.\n     2. All kinds of leave with leave salary (including Earned Leave, Half Pay Leave, Commuted Leave, Maternity Leave).\n     3. Extraordinary Leave (EOL) granted on medical certificate or for approved higher studies. (EOL on private affairs without medical grounds *does not* count for increment).\n     4. Period of joining time and foreign service.\n",
