            "id": "pc4_s1_c11",
            "chapterNum": 11,
            "title": "7. General Provident Fund (Central Services) Rules",
            "content": "### 7. General Provident Fund (Central Services) Rules\n\nThe **General Provident Fund (Central Services) Rules, 1960** regulate the statutory provident fund mechanism for civil servants who entered Central Government service on or before **31 December 2003** (prior to the introduction of the National Pension System). The GPF functions as a compulsory, interest-bearing, defined-contribution retirement savings vehicle with strict statutory safeguards against debt attachment.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Eligibility, Subscriptions and Caps\n- **Eligibility**: All permanent, temporary, and re-employed central civil government servants who joined service prior to 01.01.2004 are eligible and required to subscribe to the GPF.\n- **Subscription Rates**:\n  - **Minimum Subscription**: Not less than **6 percent** of the subscriber's monthly emoluments (Basic Pay).\n  - **Maximum Subscription**: Up to **100 percent** of monthly emoluments.\n  - *Statutory Cap on Subscriptions*: The Ministry of Finance introduced a ceiling limiting total GPF subscriptions to a maximum of **Rs 5,00,000 per financial year**. Any interest accrued on employee contributions exceeding Rs 5 lakh annually is subject to income tax under the **Income Tax Act, 1961**.\n- **Cessation of Subscription before Retirement**:\n  - Under Rule 7(4), subscription to the Fund is **mandatorily stopped during the last three months of service** immediately preceding the date of superannuation, facilitating final ledger reconciliation and timely settlement.\n  - Subscriptions are suspended during periods of suspension from duty.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Nominations: Legal Safeguards under Rule 5\n- Every subscriber must file a formal **Nomination** in the prescribed form upon joining the Fund:\n  - If the subscriber has a **family** at the time of nomination, the nomination can be made **only in favor of one or more members of his family**. Any nomination made in favor of a non-family person while a family exists is **null and void**.\n  - **Definition of \"Family\" for GPF**: Wife or wives (except judicially separated), husband, parents, children, minor brothers, unmarried sisters, and deceased son's widow and children.\n  - If no family exists, nomination may be made in favor of any person or registered trust, but becomes immediately invalid upon subsequently acquiring a family.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Recoverable Advances from GPF: Rule 12\n\n```\n                           GPF ADVANCES VS. WITHDRAWALS\n                                        |\n         +------------------------------+------------------------------+\n         |                                                             |\n   RECOVERABLE ADVANCE (RULE 12)                               NON-REFUNDABLE WITHDRAWAL (RULE 15)\n   - Temporary loan repaid in installments                      - Permanent withdrawal; no repayment\n   - Admissible at any stage of service                         - Admissible after 15 years service or\n   - Ceiling: Up to 12 months' pay or                             within 10 years of superannuation\n     3/4th of balance (whichever is less)                       - Ceiling: Up to 75% or 90% of balance\n   - Permissible purposes: Illness, higher                      - Permissible purposes: House construction,\n     education, weddings, legal expenses                          purchase of site, vehicle, major illness\n   - Recovery: In 12 to 60 equal monthly                        - No interest or recovery installments\n     installments (no interest charged)\n```\n\n- **Special Advance**: Up to **90 percent** of the balance may be sanctioned by the Head of Department for prolonged, critical illness of self or dependents.\n- **Recovery Mechanics**: Deducted monthly from the pay bill commencing with the month following the drawal of advance. A subscriber may elect to repay in fewer installments than sanctioned.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Non-Refundable Withdrawals: Rule 15\n- Permissible without recovery after completing **15 years of service** (or within 10 years of retirement age):\n  1. *Housing*: Purchase of residential plot, construction of a house, acquiring a ready-built flat, or repayment of an outstanding housing loan. Permitted up to **90 percent of the balance**.\n  2. *Higher Education*: Meeting costs of university, medical, engineering, or professional education of children. Permitted up to **75 percent of the balance**.\n  3. *Vehicle Purchase*: Purchase of motor car or motorcycle after 15 years of service.\n  4. *Retirement Preparation*: Within one year before superannuation, a subscriber may withdraw up to **90 percent** of the balance without assigning any specific reason.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Final Payment and Immunity from Attachment\n- **Final Settlement (Rule 31 to 33)**: Upon retirement, resignation, invalidation, or death of the subscriber, the balance standing to his credit, along with compound interest up to the end of the preceding month, becomes payable immediately to the subscriber or valid nominees without requiring formal administrative sanction.\n- **Legal Protection under Provident Funds Act, 1925**:\n  - Section 3 of the **Provident Funds Act, 1925** grants complete statutory immunity to compulsory deposits in the GPF:\n  - *The balance in the GPF cannot be assigned, charged, or attached under any decree or order of any Civil, Revenue, or Criminal Court in respect of any debt or liability incurred by the subscriber.*\n",
            "gist": "GPF subscription: Min 6% max 100% of basic pay. Advances are repayable in 12-36 installments. Non-refundable withdrawals allowed after 15 years service for housing/marriage.",
            "crux": "1. Minimum subscription = 6% of basic pay.\n2. Annual subscription above Rs 5 Lakh attracts tax on interest.\n3. Advances repayable in 12 to 36 monthly installments.",
