            "title": "1. Central Government Accounts (Receipts and Payment) Rules, 1983",
            "content": "### 1. Central Government Accounts (Receipts and Payment) Rules, 1983\n\nThe **Central Government Account (Receipts and Payments) Rules, 1983 [R&P Rules 1983]** were framed by the President under **Article 283(1)** of the Constitution. Following the departmentalization of Union accounts in 1976, these rules superseded the old Central Treasury Rules to regulate the receipt, custody, disbursement, and banking of public moneys across all Central Ministries and Departments.\n\n---\n\n### 1. General Principles of Receipts: Part II (Rules 6 to 25)\n- **Rule 6: Mandatory Credit of Government Dues**:\n  - All moneys received by or tendered to government servants on account of the revenues of the Union shall without undue delay be brought into account and paid in full into the **Public Account** or an **accredited bank**.\n  - *Direct Prohibition*: Money received cannot be appropriated directly to meet departmental expenditure (no \"direct utilization\" of receipts).\n- **Rule 7: Payment into Accredited Bank**:\n  - Moneys are deposited via standardized challans or through electronic gateways (e.g., **Bharatkosh / Non-Tax Receipt Portal - NTRP**).\n- **Rule 8: Issue of Receipts in Form GAR-6**:\n  - A government officer receiving cash or cheques must immediately issue a formal signed receipt in **Form GAR-6** (Treasury Form GAR-6). Receipt books are machine-numbered and kept under strict safe custody.\n- **Rule 13: Maintenance of the Cash Book (Form GAR-3)**:\n  - Every officer handling government cash must maintain a Cash Book in **Form GAR-3**.\n  - All monetary transactions must be recorded as soon as they occur and initialed by the DDO.\n  - *Prohibition*: Private money or un-cashed personal cheques must never be mixed with public cash balances.\n  - **Daily and Monthly Verification**:\n    - The Cash Book must be closed, balanced, and signed daily by the Drawing and Disbursing Officer (DDO).\n    - On the last working day of every month, the DDO must personally carry out a **physical verification of cash** and record a mandatory signed certificate:\n      *\"Certified that the cash balance has been physically verified by me and found to be Rs ... (in words and figures) which agrees with the closing balance shown in the Cash Book.\"*\n\n---\n\n### 2. Withdrawal of Money from Government Account: Part III\n- **Presentation of Bills**: No money can be withdrawn from the Consolidated Fund or Public Account except by presenting formal claims (bills) in prescribed GAR formats to the **Pay and Accounts Office (PAO)** or through authorized **Cheque-Drawing DDOs (CDDOs)**.\n- **GAR Bill Forms**:\n  - **GAR 29**: Fully Vouched Contingent Bill.\n  - **GAR 30**: Abstract Contingent (AC) Bill.\n  - **GAR 31**: Detailed Demands (DC) Bill.\n  - **GAR 36**: Travelling Allowance Bill.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Cheque Operations and Cheque-Drawing DDOs (Rules 42 to 55)\n\n```\n                            CHEQUE HANDLING RULES (RULES 42-55)\n                                             |\n         +-----------------------------------+-----------------------------------+\n         |                                                                       |\n   CHEQUE VALIDITY & CANCELLATION                                         PAYMENT CATEGORIZATION\n   - Government cheques are valid for                                    - Cheque-Drawing DDO (CDDO):\n     3 months from the date of issue (Rule 45)                             Authorized to draw cheques directly on\n   - Revalidation: Stale cheques revalidated                               accredited bank branches for letters\n     by DDO by altering date and re-signing                                of credit / assigned funds\n   - Cheque delivery register maintained                                 - Non-Cheque-Drawing DDO (NCDDO):\n     under strict supervisory custody                                      Submits bills to PAO for pre-check\n   - Lost cheques require \"Non-Payment Certificate\"                        and cheque/e-payment issuance\n     from bank before issuing fresh cheque\n```\n\n- **Lapsed Cheques (Rule 47)**: A cheque that remains uncashed beyond the financial year in which it was issued, or after more than three months from the revalidation date, is cancelled, and the amount is written back to the functional expenditure head.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Departmentalized Hierarchy and Audit Oversight\n- The administrative structure comprises:\n  1. **Principal Accounts Office (PrAO)**: Headed by Chief Controller of Accounts / Controller of Accounts in each Ministry.\n  2. **Pay and Accounts Office (PAO)**: The core pre-audit and bill-passing unit.\n  3. **Drawing and Disbursing Officer (DDO)**: Field officer preparing claims and maintaining the primary cash book.\n- **Treasury Single Account (TSA) System**: Implemented across autonomous bodies and major central departments, eliminating the holding of idle government cash in commercial bank current accounts by executing payments directly through the RBI's **e-Kuber** core banking solution.\n",
            "gist": "Receipts & Payments Rules 1983 govern prompt banking of public money, DDO bill submissions, cheque validity (3 months), and mandatory loss reporting.",
            "crux": "1. Cheque validity is strictly 3 months.\n2. Money cannot be drawn from treasury in advance of requirements.\n3. Loss of public money > Rs 10,000 must be reported immediately to AG.",
