            "id": "pc4_s2_c2",
            "chapterNum": 2,
            "title": "2. General Financial Rules, 2017 (Chapters 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 9, 10, 11 & 12)",
            "content": "### 2. General Financial Rules, 2017 (Chapters 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 9, 10, 11 & 12)\n\nThe **General Financial Rules, 2017 (GFR 2017)** issued by the Department of Expenditure, Ministry of Finance, constitute the supreme administrative code governing public financial management, budgeting, procurement, and contract administration across all Central Government Ministries and Departments.\n\n---\n\n### 1. General Principles and Cannons of Financial Propriety (Chapters 1 & 2)\n- **Rule 21: Cannons of Financial Propriety**: Every government officer incurring or authorizing public expenditure must observe four fundamental standards:\n  1. **Prudence**: The expenditure should not be prima facie more than the occasion demands. Every public officer is expected to exercise the **same vigilance in respect of expenditure incurred from public moneys as a person of ordinary prudence would exercise in respect of expenditure of his own money**.\n  2. **Sanction Authority**: The authority must exercise its delegated powers without exceeding financial limits and without self-interest.\n  3. **No Personal Benefit**: Public funds must not be utilized for the benefit of a particular person or section of the people, unless a recognized public claim exists.\n  4. **No Direct Remuneration**: The amount of allowances granted to meet expenditure of a particular type must be regulated so that it does not become a source of profit to the recipient.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Procurement of Goods and Services through GeM (Chapter 6 & Rule 149)\nThe **Government e-Marketplace (GeM)** is the mandatory online platform for procurement of common-use goods and services:\n\n```\n                            GeM PROCUREMENT THRESHOLDS (RULE 149)\n                                             |\n         +-----------------------------------+-----------------------------------+\n         |                                   |                                   |\nUP TO RS 25,000                     RS 25,000 TO RS 5,00,000            ABOVE RS 5,00,000\n- Direct purchase without           - Comparison of at least 3          - Mandatory Online Bidding\n  quotations                          different manufacturers /           or Reverse Auction (RA)\n- Any available seller on GeM         sellers on GeM                      on the GeM portal\n  meeting required quality,         - Selection of lowest price         - Strict evaluation of technical\n  specifications & delivery           (L-1) meeting specifications        specifications before opening bids\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. Alternative Procurement Modalities outside GeM\nWhere items are not available on GeM, GFR 2017 prescribes structured procurement methods:\n- **Rule 154: Purchase without Quotation**: Permissible for goods up to **Rs 25,000** on each occasion, based on a certificate of suitability recorded by the competent authority.\n- **Rule 155: Purchase by Local Purchase Committee (LPC)**: Permissible for purchases between **Rs 25,000 and Rs 2,50,000** through a three-member committee surveying the local market.\n- **Rule 158: Bid Security / Earnest Money Deposit (EMD)**: To safeguard against frivolous bids, EMD ranging between **1 percent and 3 percent** of the estimated project value is obtained. (Micro and Small Enterprises - MSEs and Startups are exempted).\n- **Rule 171: Performance Security**: Obtained from the successful bidder to guarantee contract execution, typically fixed at **3 percent to 5 percent** of the total contract value (valid for 60 days beyond completion of contractual obligations).\n- **Rule 161 & 162: Tender Types**:\n  - *Advertised Tender Enquiry (Open Tender)*: Mandatory for procurements with estimated value **above Rs 25 lakh**.\n  - *Limited Tender Enquiry*: Used for procurements up to **Rs 25 lakh**, inviting bids from at least three reputed registered suppliers.\n  - *Single Tender Enquiry (Rule 166)*: Permitted only under emergency, proprietary technology (PAC certificate), or standard equipment compatibility.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Grants-in-Aid, Loans and Inventory Management (Chapters 9, 10 & 11)\n- **Rule 238: Utilization Certificates (Form GFR 12-A)**:\n  - Grants-in-aid released to autonomous bodies, PRIs, or NGOs require the submission of a signed **Utilization Certificate (UC)** within **12 months of the closure of the financial year**.\n  - *Mandatory Sanction Bar*: No subsequent grant installment can be released to any grantee institution without receiving the audited UC for prior releases.\n- **Inventory and Asset Verification (Chapter 7)**:\n  - Fixed Asset Register maintained in **Form GFR 22** (Plant and Machinery) and **Form GFR 23** (Consumable Stores).\n  - Mandatory **annual physical verification of inventory** by an officer independent of the storekeeper (Rule 213).\n  - Condemnation and disposal of surplus/unserviceable stores executed under Rules 217 to 222 via public auction or tender.\n",
            "gist": "GFR 2017 Rule 21 establishes the Standards of Financial Propriety (vigilance of ordinary prudence). Rule 149 mandates GeM procurement. Chapter 9 regulates Grants-in-Aid.",
