            "title": "3. Delegation of Financial Powers Rules 1978",
            "content": "### 3. Delegation of Financial Powers Rules 1978\n\nThe **Delegation of Financial Powers Rules, 1978 [DFPR 1978]** were promulgated by the President under **Article 77(3)** of the Constitution to regulate the financial sanctioning powers of administrative authorities in Central Civil Ministries and Departments. By delegating expenditure authority to subordinate hierarchies while imposing statutory ceilings, the DFPR ensures that public funds are disbursed with administrative efficiency and fiscal discipline.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Hierarchy of Financial Authorities\nThe DFPR establishes a four-tier operational hierarchy:\n1. **The Administrative Ministry / Department**:\n   - Vested with wide powers within the sanctioned Parliamentary budget, subject to the advice of the **Integrated Financial Adviser (IFA)**.\n2. **Head of Department (HOD)**:\n   - Senior officers declared as HODs under Rule 2(f) (e.g., Directors General, Principal Chief Commissioners, Accountants General) possessing substantial sanctioning powers specified in **Schedules I to VII**.\n3. **Head of Office (HOO)**:\n   - A Gazetted Officer declared by the HOD under Rule 14 to manage the day-to-day administrative and financial transactions of a specific office.\n4. **Drawing and Disbursing Officer (DDO)**:\n   - Authorized to draw bills and disburse funds on behalf of the HOO.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Powers of Re-appropriation: Rule 8\n**Re-appropriation** means the transfer of savings from one unit of appropriation to another within the same voted Grant or charged Appropriation:\n\n```\n                         STATUTORY LIMITS ON RE-APPROPRIATION (RULE 8)\n                                              |\n     +----------------------------------------+----------------------------------------+\n     |                                                                                 |\nABSOLUTE STATUTORY PROHIBITIONS                                              MINISTRY-LEVEL RESTRICTIONS\n1. Cannot re-appropriate between VOTED and CHARGED                          1. Cannot re-appropriate from / to Secret-\n2. Cannot re-appropriate between REVENUE and CAPITAL                           ariat Administration without MoF consent\n3. Cannot re-appropriate funds to initiate a \"NEW SERVICE\"                  2. Cannot re-appropriate savings from\n   or \"NEW INSTRUMENT OF SERVICE\" without Parliament vote                      Externally Aided Projects (EAP)\n4. Cannot re-appropriate funds from savings in Statutory                    3. Cannot augment salary allocations\n   Grants (Art 275) to other administrative heads                              without explicit concurrence of MoF\n```\n\n- *New Service Threshold*: If an activity is categorized as a \"New Service\" or \"New Instrument of Service\" based on monetary limits fixed by the Public Accounts Committee (PAC), funds cannot be provided by re-appropriation; a Supplementary Demand under Article 115 is mandatory.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Contingent and Miscellaneous Expenditure Ceilings\n- **Contingent Expenditure (Rule 13 & Schedule V)**: Incidental expenses required for running an office (stationery, electricity, telephone, postage, repairs, IT consumables):\n  - *Recurring Contingent Expenditure*: Subject to strict annual limits prescribed in Schedule V. An HOD may sanction up to specified ceilings; higher amounts require Administrative Ministry concurrence.\n  - *Non-recurring Contingent Expenditure*: Sanctioned on each occasion up to delegated limits (e.g., local equipment purchases, minor office modifications).\n- **Miscellaneous Expenditure (Rule 15 & Schedule VI)**: Expenditures that are neither contingent nor related to public works/stores (e.g., hospitality, refreshments, civic receptions, legal fees). Governed by restrictive caps.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Role of the Integrated Financial Adviser (IFA)\n- Every Union Ministry operates an **Integrated Financial Advice (IFA) Wing** headed by an Additional Secretary / Joint Secretary (Financial Adviser):\n  - The IFA combines internal financial advice with expenditure control, participating in all expenditure proposals exceeding the delegated powers of HODs.\n  - In cases of fundamental disagreement between the Secretary of the Ministry and the IFA, the Secretary may overrule the IFA in writing, but such instances must be reported to the Ministry of Finance and the CAG.\n",
