            "title": "2. Accounting Process – Books of original entry, Journal, Ledger, BRS, Trial Balance and Rectification of Errors",
            "content": "### The Accounting Cycle: Journal to Trial Balance & Rectification\n\n1. **Double Entry System**: Debit the receiver, Credit the giver (Personal); Debit what comes in, Credit what goes out (Real); Debit all expenses and losses, Credit all incomes and gains (Nominal).\n2. **Bank Reconciliation Statement (BRS)**:\n   - Reconciles difference between Cash Book bank column and Bank Passbook.\n   - *Additions to Cash Book Balance*: Cheques issued but not presented, credits directly deposited in bank, interest credited by bank.\n   - *Deductions*: Cheques deposited but not cleared, bank charges debited, direct debits/standing orders.\n3. **Rectification of Errors**:\n   - *Errors not affecting Trial Balance*: Errors of omission, errors of commission, errors of principle (treating capital expenditure as revenue), compensating errors.\n   - *Errors affecting Trial Balance*: One-sided errors rectified through **Suspense Account**.",
            "gist": "Accounting cycle: Journal, Ledger, Trial Balance, BRS. One-sided errors are rectified using Suspense Account; errors of principle affect P&L.",
            "crux": "1. BRS reconciles timing differences between Cash Book and Passbook.\n2. Errors of principle do not affect Trial Balance totals.\n3. Suspense Account temporarily absorbs one-sided trial balance discrepancies.",
            "flashcards": [
              {
                "term": "2. Accounting Process – Books of origina",
                "def": "Accounting cycle: Journal, Ledger, Trial Balance, BRS. One-sided errors are rectified using Suspense Account; errors of "
              }
            ],
