            "chapterNum": 5,
            "title": "(v) Submission of Draft Accounts to Accountant General (Audit)",
            "content": "### (v) Submission of Draft Accounts to Accountant General (Audit)\n\nThe compilation and certification of the annual accounts of the State Government—comprising the **Finance Accounts** and the **Appropriation Accounts**—involves an institutional workflow between the **Principal Accountant General / Accountant General (Accounts & Entitlements) [PAG/AG (A&E)]** and the **Principal Accountant General / Accountant General (Audit) [PAG/AG (Audit)]**. Regulation 24 of the **Regulations on Audit and Accounts 2020** establishes the procedural mechanisms for the submission, scrutiny, and joint clearance of draft annual accounts.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Statutory Mandate and Target Timelines\nUnder **Section 10 and Section 11 of the CAG's DPC Act, 1971**, the CAG compiles the accounts of the State, and submits the certified accounts along with audit reports to the Governor under **Article 151(2)** of the Constitution:\n- **Draft Accounts Preparation**:\n  - The AG (A&E) prepares the initial draft of the annual **Finance Accounts (Volumes I & II)** and **Appropriation Accounts** from compiled monthly civil accounts, treasury ledgers, and departmentally reconciled figures.\n- **Strict Annual Timelines**:\n  - Draft accounts are targeted for completion and formal submission to the AG (Audit) by **30th June / 31st July** following the close of the financial year.\n  - Adherence to timelines is vital to ensure that printed, certified accounts can be submitted to the State Government before the commencement of the monsoon or winter session of the State Legislature.\n\n---\n\n### 2. The Structured Audit Scrutiny Workflow\n\n```\n                        DRAFT ACCOUNTS SUBMISSION & SCRUTINY\n                                          |\n         +--------------------------------+--------------------------------+\n         |                                                                 |\nAG (A&E) RESPONSIBILITIES                                           AG (AUDIT) SCRUTINY ACTIONS\n- Finalizes Statements of Finance Accounts (1 to 22)                 - Vets draft statements against original sanctions\n- Compares actual expenditure with final grants (Voted/Charged)     - Verifies un-reconciled treasury differences\n- Prepares Explanatory Notes on Savings & Excesses                  - Issues Audit Queries / Management Letter to A&E\n- Submits draft accounts to AG (Audit) with working sheets          - Conducts joint reconciliation meeting to resolve gaps\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. Key Scrutiny Checkpoints Examined by Audit\nThe Audit Wing subjects the draft accounts to rigorous analytical and compliance testing:\n1. **Appropriation Accounts Scrutiny**:\n   - Verifying that original budget estimates, supplementary grants under **Article 205**, and re-appropriations under **DFPR** match the official State Budget and Appropriation Acts passed by the Legislative Assembly.\n   - Examining the validity of explanations provided for significant **Savings** or **Excesses** exceeding prescribed monetary and percentage thresholds (e.g., variations exceeding Rs 50 lakh or 10 percent of the grant).\n   - Verifying that no expenditure was incurred without budget provision or under unauthorized re-appropriation.\n2. **Finance Accounts Scrutiny**:\n   - *Suspense Balances (Major Head 8658)*: Verifying whether uncleared suspense balances have been correctly explained in footnote disclosures.\n   - *Adverse Balances*: Investigating debit balances under receipt/liability heads or credit balances under asset/expenditure heads.\n   - *Debt Servicing & Market Loans*: Verifying whether public debt figures match Reserve Bank of India (RBI) confirmation statements.\n   - *Off-Budget Borrowings*: Verifying whether guarantees issued by the State Government under **Article 293** and liabilities incurred by State PSUs/SPVs are fully disclosed.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Management Letter, Joint Reconciliation and Final Sign-Off\n- **Issue of Audit Observations**: Following review, the AG (Audit) issues formal audit observations or a **Management Letter** to the AG (A&E) highlighting discrepancies, mathematical errors, or incomplete footnote disclosures.\n- **Joint Reconciliation Meetings**: Joint meetings are convened between the Book Section of the A&E office and the Financial Audit / Report Section of the Audit office to resolve outstanding queries.\n- **Audit Certificate**: Once all corrections are incorporated, the AG (Audit) finalizes the **Audit Certificate** (to be signed by the CAG of India) stating whether the accounts present a true and fair view in accordance with applicable accounting standards.\n",
            "gist": "Draft accounts submitted to AG (Audit) by 31st July. Audited concurrently to finalize Audit Certificate and explanatory footnotes.",
            "crux": "1. Formal cutoff date: 31st July.\n2. AG (Audit) conducts audit of accounts under Section 13 of DPC Act.\n3. Discrepancies resolved via joint coordination committees.",
