            "id": "pc11_s1_c6",
            "chapterNum": 6,
            "title": "(vi) Certificate of the A.G. (A&E) & Certificate of the A.G. (Audit) including emphasis of matter",
            "content": "### Certificates on Annual Accounts: A&E Compilation vs Audit Certification\n\n1. **Certificate of the AG (A&E)**:\n   - Signed by the Accountant General (A&E).\n   - Certifies that the accounts have been compiled from the records and accounts submitted by Treasuries, Pay and Accounts Offices, and Departmental Officers, and are correct according to the best of information available.\n2. **Audit Certificate of the AG (Audit) / CAG**:\n   - Signed by the C&AG of India (tabled before Legislature).\n   - Gives an audit opinion on whether the accounts give a True and Fair view in all material respects.\n3. **Emphasis of Matter (EoM)**:\n   - Highlights critical accounting distortions without modifying the overall opinion (e.g. substantial un-reconciled differences with RBI, booking of expenditure under suspense without vouchers, failure to adjust AC bills into DC bills, massive unspent funds parked in bank accounts outside the Consolidated Fund).",
            "gist": "AG (A&E) certifies compilation accuracy. CAG signs the formal Audit Certificate. Emphasis of Matter highlights major distortions like parked funds or un-adjusted AC bills.",
            "crux": "1. AG (A&E) certificate guarantees compilation integrity.\n2. CAG signs constitutional audit report under Article 151.\n3. Emphasis of Matter highlights major control failures like pending DC bills.",
