            "id": "pc11_s4_c5",
            "chapterNum": 5,
            "title": "(v) Asset Accounting – tables and templates",
            "content": "### (v) Asset Accounting – tables and templates\n\nTraditional government accounting in India operates on a **cash basis**, recording capital expenditure as final outlays in the year of disbursement without maintaining an integrated commercial balance sheet. To capture public assets, enforce stewardship over state infrastructure, and transition toward modern accounting standards, the **Government Accounting Standards Advisory Board (GASAB)** under the **Comptroller and Auditor General of India (CAG)** formulated structured guidelines, tables, and reporting templates for **Asset Accounting**.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Conceptual Classification of Government Assets\nIn public accounts, government assets are categorized into three distinct classes:\n\n```\n                            CLASSIFICATION OF GOVERNMENT ASSETS\n                                             |\n         +-----------------------------------+-----------------------------------+\n         |                                   |                                   |\nPHYSICAL / FIXED ASSETS             FINANCIAL ASSETS                    INTANGIBLE ASSETS\n- Land, administrative buildings    - Equity investments in State       - Software applications & ERPs\n- Roads, highways, and bridges        PSUs, corporations & banks        - Mining rights & spectrum\n- Irrigation dams & canals          - Loans and advances to local       - Patents & intellectual\n- Plant, machinery, and equipment     bodies, SPVs, and civil servants    property rights\n```\n\n- **Capital Works-in-Progress (CWIP)**: Incomplete engineering works where expenditure has been incurred under Capital Outlay Major Heads (`4059`, `4215`, `5054`) but physical assets are yet to be commissioned.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Asset Statements in the Annual Finance Accounts\nThe annual **State Finance Accounts** compiled by the Accountant General (A&E) incorporate four statutory statements detailing the Government's assets:\n1. **Statement 4: Summary of Capital Expenditure and Loans & Advances**:\n   - Presents a macro-level summary of capital outlays during the year, progressive capital expenditure to end of the year, investments in statutory corporations, and net loans advanced.\n2. **Statement 16: Detailed Statement of Capital Outlay**:\n   - Comprehensive statement showing progressive capital expenditure by **Major, Minor, and Sub-Heads** across all developmental sectors.\n   - Discloses capital investments in irrigation projects, power plants, hospital buildings, and highway networks.\n3. **Statement 19: Detailed Statement of Investments (Financial Assets)**:\n   - Documents the State Government's equity investments in:\n     - Statutory Corporations (e.g., State Road Transport Corporation).\n     - Government Companies (State Power Generation / Distribution Companies).\n     - Joint Stock Companies and Co-operative Institutions.\n   - Tracks paid-up share value, number of shares held, percentage of government equity holding, and dividend received during the year.\n4. **Statement 20: Detailed Statement of Loans and Advances Given by Government**:\n   - Records loans disbursed to Panchayati Raj Institutions, Municipalities, State PSUs, and housing advances to government servants, detailing opening balances, repayments, write-offs, and overdue interest.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Registers and Templates under General Financial Rules (GFR 2017)\nAt the administrative department and DDO level, physical asset custody is maintained through standardized registers:\n- **Form GFR 22: Fixed Assets Register (Plant, Machinery & Equipment)**:\n  - Captures: Description of asset, purchase voucher number and date, supplier details, cost of acquisition, location/custodian, physical verification dates, and disposal particulars.\n- **Form GFR 23: Register of Consumable Stores**:\n  - Captures receipt, issues, and closing balances of consumable supplies (stationery, spare parts, fuel).\n- **Rule 213 Verification**: Mandatory **annual physical verification of all fixed assets** by an independent verification officer, with discrepancies investigated and reported to the Head of Department.\n\n---\n\n### 4. GASAB Road Map: Transition to Accrual and Depreciation\n- Under traditional cash accounting, capital assets are written off as expenditure upon payment, and **no depreciation** is charged in general civil accounts.\n- **GASAB Reforms**:\n  - GASAB has drafted **Indian Government Financial Reporting Standards (IGFRS)** to operationalize accrual-based balance sheets.\n  - Proposed standards mandate formal valuation of land, historical cost recording of infrastructure assets, impairment testing, and structured depreciation accounting across the public sector.\n",
            "gist": "GASAB templates standardize physical asset tracking, monetary valuation, revenue reconciliation, and DMF social expenditure reporting.",
            "crux": "1. Standardized templates drafted by GASAB for all States.\n2. Table 1 tracks physical volume; Table 2 tracks monetary valuation.\n3. Table 3 identifies revenue leakages and illegal mining.",
