            "content": "### Constitutional Framework for Budgeting and Appropriation\n\n1. **Union Provisions (Articles 112–117)**:\n   - *Article 112*: Annual Financial Statement laid before Parliament.\n   - *Article 113*: Distinction between Charged expenditure (non-votable) and Voted expenditure (Demands for Grants).\n   - *Article 114*: Appropriation Bill — no withdrawal from Consolidated Fund without legislative enactment.\n   - *Article 115*: Supplementary, additional or excess grants.\n   - *Article 116*: Vote on Account.\n2. **State Provisions (Articles 202–207)**: Identical structure for State Governments and State Legislative Assemblies.\n3. **Charged vs Voted Expenditure**:\n   - *Charged*: Emoluments of President, Governors, Judges of Supreme Court and High Courts, CAG, Debt servicing charges; non-votable by Legislature.\n   - *Voted*: Subject to voting and cut motions in the lower House.",
            "gist": "Articles 112-117 (Union) and 202-207 (States) mandate Annual Financial Statement, Demands for Grants, and Appropriation Acts. Charged expenditure is non-votable.",
            "crux": "1. No expenditure can be incurred without Appropriation Act (Art 114/204).\n2. Charged expenditure can be discussed but not voted.\n3. Excess expenditure must be regularized under Art 115/205 via PAC.",
