            "chapterNum": 1,
            "title": "a. Objective of FRBM Act 2003",
            "content": "### a. Objective of FRBM Act 2003\n\nThe **Fiscal Responsibility and Budget Management Act, 2003 [FRBM Act, 2003]** (Act No. 39 of 2003) constitutes the legislative cornerstone of macroeconomic discipline and fiscal consolidation in India. Enacted by Parliament in August 2003 and notified w.e.f. **5 July 2004**, the Act establishes a statutory framework binding the Central Government to prudent fiscal management, inter-generational equity, debt sustainability, and fiscal transparency.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Legislative Genesis and Historical Context\n- **The Fiscal Crisis of the 1990s and Early 2000s**:\n  - Following economic liberalization, India experienced persistent, unsustainable fiscal imbalances:\n    - Gross Fiscal Deficit of the Centre averaged over **5.5 to 6.5 percent of GDP**.\n    - Revenue Deficit hovered around **3.5 to 4.5 percent of GDP**, meaning the Government was borrowing heavily merely to meet day-to-day consumption expenditures.\n    - Public debt-to-GDP ratios approached 80%, threatening macroeconomic stability and crowding out private investment.\n- **Enactment**: Parliament legislated the FRBM Act to legally mandate that the Executive eliminate revenue deficits and place borrowing on a declining, sustainable trajectory.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Core Statutory Objectives of the FRBM Act\nThe Act articulates six foundational statutory objectives:\n\n```\n                            CORE OBJECTIVES OF FRBM ACT, 2003\n                                            |\n         +----------------------------------+----------------------------------+\n         |                                  |                                  |\n1. FISCAL DISCIPLINE & STABILITY   2. ELIMINATION OF REVENUE DEFICIT  3. INTER-GENERATIONAL EQUITY\n- Contain fiscal deficits within   - Ensure current revenues finance  - Prevent current generations\n  prudent macroeconomic limits       current consumption expenses       from passing debt burdens\n- Prevent inflationary pressures   - Dedicate all borrowed moneys       to future generations\n  and macroeconomic volatility       strictly to productive assets      without productive assets\n\n4. GREATER BUDGET TRANSPARENCY     5. DEBT SUSTAINABILITY             6. MONETARY-FISCAL SEPARATION\n- Eliminate opaque off-budget      - Cap total outstanding debt       - End automatic monetization of\n  liabilities and hidden debt        within prudent debt-to-GDP         deficits via ad-hoc Treasury Bills;\n- Mandate statutory disclosures      thresholds                         bar RBI from primary debt market\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. Prohibition of Deficit Monetization: Section 5\nA transformative structural reform introduced by Section 5 of the Act:\n- **Ending Automatic Monetization**: Ended the practice of the Government issuing ad-hoc Treasury Bills to the Reserve Bank of India (RBI) to fund deficits automatically through money creation.\n- **Bar on Primary Debt Purchases**: W.e.f. **1 April 2006**, the Reserve Bank of India was **statutorily prohibited from subscribing to primary issuances of Central Government securities (G-Secs)**. The Central Government must raise loans exclusively through competitive market auctions, subjecting public debt to market pricing.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Mandatory Statutory Statements to Parliament: Section 3\nTo guarantee fiscal transparency, Section 3 mandates that the Central Government lay four statutory statements before both Houses of Parliament along with the Annual Financial Statement:\n1. **Macro-economic Framework Statement**: Assesses GDP growth, balance of payments, inflation, and external sector stability.\n2. **Medium-Term Fiscal Policy Statement (MTFPS)**: Sets three-year rolling targets for Revenue Deficit, Fiscal Deficit, Tax-to-GDP ratio, and Total Debt.\n3. **Fiscal Policy Strategy Statement (FPSS)**: Outlines executive policy priorities regarding taxation, expenditure rationalization, and subsidy administration.\n4. **Medium-Term Expenditure Framework Statement (MTEF)**: Sets three-year rolling expenditure ceilings for functional ministries and developmental programs.\n\n---\n\n### 5. The NK Singh Committee (2016-17) and the \"Escape Clause\"\n- **NK Singh Review Committee**:\n  - Recommended shifting primary focus from the annual fiscal deficit to the **General Government Debt-to-GDP ratio**:\n    - Recommended target of **60 percent of GDP** (40% Central Government + 20% State Governments).\n    - Recommended annual fiscal deficit ceiling of **2.5 percent of GDP** by FY 2023.\n- **The Statutory Escape Clause (Section 4(2))**:\n  - Permits the Central Government to deviate from annual fiscal deficit targets by up to **0.5 percent of GDP** on specific, narrowly defined statutory grounds:\n    1. Act of war or threats to national security.\n    2. National calamity of severe magnitude.\n    3. Collapse of agriculture affecting national food security.\n    4. Far-reaching structural reforms with unanticipated fiscal implications (e.g., GST rollout).\n    5. Sharp decline in real GDP growth (fall of at least 3 percentage points below previous four quarters).\n- **Role of the CAG**: The CAG conducts independent audit reviews of FRBM compliance statements presented to Parliament, scrutinizing disclosures, identifying concealed liabilities, and calculating true fiscal metrics.\n",
