            "content": "### Treasury Inspection vs Audit of IFMS: Comparative Analysis\n\n1. **Treasury Inspection**:\n   - *Focus*: Examination of procedural, statutory, and operational compliance at an individual district/sub-treasury office.\n   - *Scope*: Physical verification of cash/stamps in Strong Room, custody of valuable deposit registers, verification of pension disburser halves, checking whether bills are paid as per treasury rules.\n   - *Standard*: Accounting Rules for Treasuries 1992 and Section 12 of DPC Act 1971.\n2. **Audit of IFMS (Information Systems Audit)**:\n   - *Focus*: Comprehensive evaluation of the centralized state software application itself.\n   - *Scope*: IT general controls (GITC), database security, business application logic, change management, encryption of e-payment files, vulnerability assessment, and API security with RBI e-Kuber.\n   - *Standard*: CAG's IT Audit Manual 2024 and WGITA-IDI guidelines.",
            "gist": "Treasury Inspection evaluates field procedural compliance, physical cash, and pension records at a local treasury. Audit of IFMS is an IS audit evaluating the core software and database architecture.",
            "crux": "1. Treasury Inspection checks physical records, strong room, and pension files.\n2. Audit of IFMS audits the central software code, database, and IT controls.\n3. Treasury Inspection conducted under DPC Act Sec 12; IFMS Audit under Sec 13.",
